{"entities":"[{\"text\": \"scopolamine\", \"id\": \"D012601\", \"label\": \"Chemical\"}, {\"text\": \"amnesia\", \"id\": \"D000647\", \"label\": \"Disease\"}, {\"text\": \"naloxone\", \"id\": \"D009270\", \"label\": \"Chemical\"}, {\"text\": \"naloxone\", \"id\": \"D009270\", \"label\": \"Chemical\"}, {\"text\": \"scopolamine\", \"id\": \"D012601\", \"label\": \"Chemical\"}, {\"text\": \"retention deficit\", \"id\": \"D008569\", \"label\": \"Disease\"}, {\"text\": \"Scopolamine\", \"id\": \"D012601\", \"label\": \"Chemical\"}, {\"text\": \"methyl scopolamine\", \"id\": \"D019832\", \"label\": \"Chemical\"}, {\"text\": \"amnesia\", \"id\": \"D000647\", \"label\": \"Disease\"}, {\"text\": \"Naloxone\", \"id\": \"D009270\", \"label\": \"Chemical\"}, {\"text\": \"retention deficit\", \"id\": \"D008569\", \"label\": \"Disease\"}, {\"text\": \"naloxone\", \"id\": \"D009270\", \"label\": \"Chemical\"}, {\"text\": \"morphine\", \"id\": \"D009020\", \"label\": \"Chemical\"}, {\"text\": \"naloxone\", \"id\": \"D009270\", \"label\": \"Chemical\"}, {\"text\": \"naloxone\", \"id\": \"D009270\", \"label\": \"Chemical\"}, {\"text\": \"scopolamine\", \"id\": \"D012601\", \"label\": \"Chemical\"}, {\"text\": \"amnesia\", \"id\": \"D000647\", \"label\": \"Disease\"}, {\"text\": \"pain\", \"id\": \"D010146\", \"label\": \"Disease\"}, {\"text\": \"naloxone\", \"id\": \"D009270\", \"label\": \"Chemical\"}, {\"text\": \"naloxone\", \"id\": \"D009270\", \"label\": \"Chemical\"}, {\"text\": \"naloxone\", \"id\": \"D009270\", \"label\": \"Chemical\"}, {\"text\": \"scopolamine\", \"id\": \"D012601\", \"label\": \"Chemical\"}, {\"text\": \"retention deficit\", \"id\": \"D008569\", \"label\": \"Disease\"}]","filtered_entities":[{"text":"scopolamine","id":"D012601","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"amnesia","id":"D000647","label":"Disease"},{"text":"naloxone","id":"D009270","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"retention deficit","id":"D008569","label":"Disease"},{"text":"methyl scopolamine","id":"D019832","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"morphine","id":"D009020","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"pain","id":"D010146","label":"Disease"}],"head":"morphine","head_id":"D009020","pairs":[{"head":"scopolamine","tail":"amnesia","head_id":"D012601","tail_id":"D000647"},{"head":"scopolamine","tail":"retention deficit","head_id":"D012601","tail_id":"D008569"},{"head":"scopolamine","tail":"pain","head_id":"D012601","tail_id":"D010146"},{"head":"naloxone","tail":"amnesia","head_id":"D009270","tail_id":"D000647"},{"head":"naloxone","tail":"retention deficit","head_id":"D009270","tail_id":"D008569"},{"head":"naloxone","tail":"pain","head_id":"D009270","tail_id":"D010146"},{"head":"methyl scopolamine","tail":"amnesia","head_id":"D019832","tail_id":"D000647"},{"head":"methyl scopolamine","tail":"retention deficit","head_id":"D019832","tail_id":"D008569"},{"head":"methyl scopolamine","tail":"pain","head_id":"D019832","tail_id":"D010146"},{"head":"morphine","tail":"amnesia","head_id":"D009020","tail_id":"D000647"},{"head":"morphine","tail":"retention deficit","head_id":"D009020","tail_id":"D008569"},{"head":"morphine","tail":"pain","head_id":"D009020","tail_id":"D010146"}],"relations":["D012601 | D000647","D012601 | D008569"],"result":"~","tail":"pain","tail_id":"D010146","text":"Reversal of scopolamine-induced amnesia of passive avoidance by pre- and post-training naloxone. In a series of five experiments, the modulating role of naloxone on a scopolamine-induced retention deficit in a passive avoidance paradigm was investigated in mice. Scopolamine, but not methyl scopolamine (1 and 3 mg/kg), induced an amnesia as measured by latency and duration parameters. Naloxone (0.3, 1, 3, and 10 mg/kg) injected prior to training attenuated the retention deficit with a peak of activity at 3 mg/kg. The effect of naloxone could be antagonized with morphine (1, 3, and 10 mg/kg), demonstrating the opioid specificity of the naloxone effect. Post-training administration of naloxone (3 mg/kg) as a single or as a split dose also attenuated the scopolamine-induced amnesia. Control experiments indicated that neither an increase in pain sensitivity (pre-training naloxone) nor an induced aversive state (post-training naloxone) appear to be responsible for the influence of naloxone on the scopolamine-induced retention deficit. These results extend previous findings implicating a cholinergic-opioid interaction in memory processes. A possible mechanism for this interaction involving the septo-hippocampal cholinergic pathway is discussed.","metrics":{"precision":0.5,"recall":1.0,"f1":0.6666666666666666,"rel_tp":1,"rel_fp":1,"rel_fn":0}}